{"kind":"star_garnet","sub_kind":"star_garnet","made":"2026-11-17 02:25","best_odds":"Fair","t":["Moderate","Fair","loose slope","residual lag","No","no","St. Joe National Forest: the Forest Service says garnets cannot be collected outside the Emerald Creek Garnet Area recreation site (281 Gulch, Forest Road 447) or while the site is closed. The site is by reservation (recreation.gov), 2 pounds of garnet a person, one permit a year, tools provided; it closed early in 2026 for lack of excavated gravel; the land rules do not allow collecting: ask the USFS office whether special permission is possible","gentle ground on the source itself, where the soil is made from the rock. The stone weathers out and stays on top while the soft rock washes away: walk slowly over the loose stones on the surface, the bare patches and the washes. A big area: start at the road point and work outward.","broken, angular pieces (within about 3 km of the source; Attal & Lave 2009)","The Emerald Creek Garnet Area opens \"Thursday through Saturday beginning the Thursday of Memorial Day weekend and ending the Saturday of Labor Day weekend\", by reservation (recreation.gov 2026); it closed early in 2026. Private ground needs the owner's OK.","Moderate or better potential for star garnet covers under 0.1% of the land and holds 2 of 2 record finds (100%; 2479.5 times the random-ground baseline); seen while v2 was built, so not a test. A small test: read it as a rough guide","nearest confirmed star garnet find: 2 km away (Emerald Creek)","Schist and phyllite of the Wallace Formation (Ysw)","{\"outside\":\"Rounded 12-sided (dodecahedron) or 24-sided (trapezohedron) crystals \\\"from sand sized particles to golf balls or larger\\\" (USFS 2026); most are under 2 cm (Evans & Moyle 2006 p.30). Many have fractured edges and an iron-oxide shell, and some are so weathered they crumble (p.30). The big ones sit in the colluvium and streams of the East Fork of Emerald Creek (Dickison 2018 p.38).\",\"colour\":\"Pink to dark red, purple and dark maroon; \\\"Some dark-red to reddish-black opaque garnets have the pronounced multirayed asterism of star garnet\\\" (Evans & Moyle p.30). The translucent purplish-red ones cut star cabochons; the transparent deep red ones are faceted (USGS SP14-95).\",\"checks\":\"The star does not show in the rough: \\\"Only cutting and polishing a rounded surface on the crystal will reveal a star if present\\\" (Dake 1938 p.16). Keep single crystals about half an inch or bigger, \\\"best\\\" for cutting (p.16). Four-ray stars are the common kind; six-ray stars are rare (Hwang et al. 2015).\",\"not_it\":\"In the same gravel: kyanite (the main impurity in the garnet sand, Crandall 1951), staurolite \\\"as large as 1 cm\\\" in Carpenter Creek tailings (Evans & Moyle p.28), ilmenite and magnetite.\",\"care\":\"Weathered garnets can crumble (Evans & Moyle p.30): carry them wrapped, and let a lapidary judge which will cut.\"}","heavy","hollow","gulch floor: the floor of a small gulch cut into the sapphire ground: \"erosion of colluvium and stream action concentrated sapphires in three major gulches\" (Berg 2014 p.1); dig to bedrock (star garnet: the garnet gravel lies on lakebeds in the gulches, and the old gem pits are cuts: Evans & Moyle 2006 p.26-28)","slope","[\"lidar 1 m (3DEP), photo NAIP 2025-07-18\",\"pins on land graded Go or easier; the card's land rule still applies here: St. Joe National Forest: the Forest Service says garnets cannot be collected outside the Emerald Creek Garnet Area recreation site (281 Gulch, Forest Road 447) or while the site is closed. The site is by reservation (recreation.gov), 2 pounds of garnet a person, one permit a year, tools provided\",\"sapphire ground in patch: gulch floor 45,157 m2, old cuts 101 m2, terrace risers 0 m2\",\"bare = NDVI below 0.35 (Otsu cut in this patch)\"]","Photo check (measured from the newest air photo): plants cover 85% or more of the ground within 100 ft of every pin (closed forest, thick brush or a wet meadow), so little bare ground shows; look for a road bank, a stream bank or a slide.","The nearest confirmed find is 1.2 mi away. This spot is the same kind of ground as the find, not the find itself.","rock face","face","an outcrop of the source rock. Look for seams and fillings, and pick over what has fallen at its foot.","nearest confirmed star garnet find: 2.4 km away (Emerald Creek)","valley","[\"lidar 1 m (3DEP), photo NAIP 2025-07-18\",\"pins on land graded Go or easier; the card's land rule still applies here: St. Joe National Forest: the Forest Service says garnets cannot be collected outside the Emerald Creek Garnet Area recreation site (281 Gulch, Forest Road 447) or while the site is closed. The site is by reservation (recreation.gov), 2 pounds of garnet a person, one permit a year, tools provided\",\"sapphire ground in patch: gulch floor 305 m2, old cuts 0 m2, terrace risers 0 m2\",\"bare = NDVI below 0.35 (Otsu cut in this patch)\"]","Photo check (measured from the newest air photo): plants cover 81% or more of the ground within 100 ft of the pin (closed forest, thick brush or a wet meadow), so little bare ground shows; look for a road bank, a stream bank or a slide.","The nearest confirmed find is 1.5 mi away. This spot is the same kind of ground as the find, not the find itself.","road cut","a road cut through the source rock. Look along the foot of the cut and the loose rock below it; stay off the road.","nearest confirmed star garnet find: 2.1 km away (Emerald Creek)","[\"lidar 1 m (3DEP), photo NAIP 2025-07-18\",\"pins on land graded Go or easier; the card's land rule still applies here: St. Joe National Forest: the Forest Service says garnets cannot be collected outside the Emerald Creek Garnet Area recreation site (281 Gulch, Forest Road 447) or while the site is closed. The site is by reservation (recreation.gov), 2 pounds of garnet a person, one permit a year, tools provided\",\"sapphire ground in patch: gulch floor 1,426 m2, old cuts 0 m2, terrace risers 0 m2\",\"bare = NDVI below 0.35 (Otsu cut in this patch)\"]","Photo check (measured from the newest air photo): about 110 ft of open water lies between the road point and the pins; find the crossing before you go.","The nearest confirmed find is 1.3 mi away. This spot is the same kind of ground as the find, not the find itself.","nearest confirmed star garnet find: 2.3 km away (Emerald Creek)","gentle bare ground on the source: no gulch, cut or terrace edge in this patch; dig the soil down to the weathered rock (Wells 1969 ranks residual and eluvial ground first) (star garnet: the garnet gravel lies on lakebeds in the gulches, and the old gem pits are cuts: Evans & Moyle 2006 p.26-28)","[\"lidar 1 m (3DEP), photo NAIP 2025-07-18 (gaps filled from 2021)\",\"pins on land graded Go or easier; the card's land rule still applies here: St. Joe National Forest: the Forest Service says garnets cannot be collected outside the Emerald Creek Garnet Area recreation site (281 Gulch, Forest Road 447) or while the site is closed. The site is by reservation (recreation.gov), 2 pounds of garnet a person, one permit a year, tools provided\",\"sapphire ground in patch: gulch floor 0 m2, old cuts 0 m2, terrace risers 0 m2\",\"bare = NDVI below 0.35 (Otsu cut in this patch)\"]","The nearest confirmed find is 1.4 mi away. This spot is the same kind of ground as the find, not the find itself.","river bar","first deposition zone","Permission","ask","no public land mapped here (PAD-US): private, ask the owner; you need permission first: ask the owner, claimant or manager","where the stream leaves its steep stretch and drops its load. Walk the coarse gravel in the channel and on its banks, starting where the slope eases.","Best at low water after the spring high water has moved fresh gravel.","nearest confirmed star garnet find: 2.1 km away (Emerald Creek Garnet Area)","footslope","the upstream end (head) of a bare gravel bar beside the channel: bar heads are about a third coarser than tails (Rice & Church 2010)","ridge","[\"lidar 1 m (3DEP), photo NAIP 2025-07-18\",\"pins on land graded Go or easier\",\"bare = NDVI below 0.22 (Otsu cut in this patch)\"]","bar","a gravel bar. Start at its upstream end (the head), where the coarsest stones sit, and walk the bar top; skip the sandy tail.","a confirmed star garnet find is on record near here (Emerald Creek Garnet Area); its place is only loosely known","[\"lidar 1 m (3DEP), photo NAIP 2025-07-18 (gaps filled from 2021)\",\"pins on land graded Go or easier\",\"bare = NDVI below 0.21 (Otsu cut in this patch)\"]","The nearest confirmed find is 0.1 mi away, and it is placed only to within 1.2 mi. This spot is the same kind of ground as the find, not the find itself.","dry creek bed","a gravel bar. Start at its upstream end (the head), where the coarsest stones sit, and walk the bar top; skip the sandy tail. The stream map (USGS NHD) shows a creek that holds water for only part of the year.","nearest confirmed star garnet find: 2.9 km away (Emerald Creek Garnet Area)","[\"lidar 1 m (3DEP), photo NAIP 2025-07-18\",\"pins on land graded Permission or easier\",\"bare = NDVI below 0.18 (Otsu cut in this patch)\"]","The nearest confirmed find is 1.8 mi away. This spot is the same kind of ground as the find, not the find itself.","nearest confirmed star garnet find: 9.3 km away (Emerald Creek)","gentle ground on the source itself, where the soil is made from the rock. The stone weathers out and stays on top while the soft rock washes away: walk slowly over the loose stones on the surface, the bare patches and the washes.","nearest confirmed star garnet find: 4.7 km away (Emerald Creek)","nearest confirmed star garnet find: 7.5 km away (Emerald Creek Garnet Area)","nearest confirmed star garnet find: 4.4 km away (Emerald Creek Garnet Area)","nearest confirmed star garnet find: 4.5 km away (Emerald Creek)","creek bed","where the stream leaves its steep stretch and drops its load. Walk 